    "body": "Thanks for the feedback, @foolip! Answers to your three questions:\n\n**1. Explicit schemes (`http://ejemplo.com`), allowing more than one scheme**\n\nThe proposal is that the attribute acts as a **completion default**, not a constraint. Completion applies only when the sanitized value has no scheme. Any explicitly-schemed value passes through unchanged and is validated exactly as `type=url` validates today (i.e. any scheme is allowed), so no \"allowlist\" mechanism is needed. Restricting the scheme (e.g. forbidding `http://`) would be a separate, orthogonal feature; I'd keep completion and restriction out of each other for a minimal v1.\n\n**2. Leading/trailing whitespace (` ejemplo.com `)**\n\nAlready covered by existing machinery: the URL state value sanitization algorithm (\"Strip newlines from the value, then strip leading and trailing ASCII whitespace from the value\") runs on every value change, including paste. Completion would operate on the already-sanitized value, so no new rule is needed, same as ` https://ejemplo.com ` validating today.\n\n**3. Does `input.value = 'ejemplo.com'` immediately return `'https://ejemplo.com'`?**\n\nMy recommendation is **no**: completion should *not* be part of the value sanitization algorithm. Sanitization runs on every mutation, including each keystroke: typing `e` would immediately become `https://e`, breaking caret behavior and making the completed prefix impossible to delete (effectively an immutable inline prefix, i.e. the complex #10314 UI with worse semantics).\n\nInstead, completion would be defined in two places:\n\n- **Constraint validation**: validity is computed over the completed value (so `ejemplo.com` passes when the default scheme is declared).\n- **Form data set construction**: the submitted value is the completed URL.\n\nConsequence: `.value` keeps what the user actually typed. This does mean the submitted value can differ from `.value`, but there is precedent for that divergence (`type=file`: FormData gets the `File`, while `.value` returns a fake path).",
    "author_association": "NONE",
