    "updated_at": "2026-11-13T18:06:30Z",
    "body": "Thanks @foolip, and to pin down the decomposition precisely: the proposal keeps completion distinct from the **value sanitization algorithm** (that's the part I'd argue against), while still applying it *at* validation and submission. The value sanitization algorithm runs on every value change, including each keystroke, running completion there would turn `e` into `https://e` mid-typing, breaking caret behavior and making the completed prefix impossible to delete.\n\nIn spec terms, the proposal introduces a new concept with three touchpoints:\n\n1. **A new definition**, e.g. the \"completed value\" for URL state inputs: if the element has a declared default scheme and the sanitized value has no scheme, the completed value is that scheme + `://` + the sanitized value; otherwise it is the sanitized value.\n2. **Constraint validation**: the URL state sentence (\"While the value of the element is neither the empty string nor a valid absolute URL...\") would operate on the **completed value** instead of the value.\n3. **Form data set construction**: the entry value for the element would be the completed value (analogous to how the file state uses the selected files rather than `value`).\n\nSo: no change to the value sanitization algorithm itself, the sanitized value and `.value` keep reflecting what the user typed. (The alternative model, funneling completion through sanitization so `.value` always returns the completed URL, hits the keystroke problem above; that's why I'd rather introduce the completed-value concept...)\n\nThanks",
